From 0c6c86decc6116094a3a2e9f634bde4317e421dc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nan Xiao <nanxstats@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 21:22:48 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] Add Gauss-Legendre quadrature as an opt-in integration
 method

The recursive integration of Jennison & Turnbull (2000, Chapter 19)
integrates analytic functions (sub-densities times normal kernels or tail
probabilities) over the continuation region of each analysis. Gauss-Legendre
quadrature converges geometrically for such integrands, whereas Simpson's
rule on the Jennison & Turnbull grid converges as m^-4. This adds

* src/gsquad.c: Gauss-Legendre rules for a fixed menu of node counts (8 to
  992), computed once on first use by Newton iteration on Legendre
  polynomials and kept in static storage; glpts() maps a rule onto the
  continuation region (an infinite bound is replaced by mu +/- 8, where the
  normal tail is below 1e-15); gsgrid() dispatches between the two schemes.
  The node count adapts to the width of the region relative to the kernel
  width sqrt(dI / I) of the smaller of the information increments into and
  out of the analysis (the sub-density is as sharp as the kernel that
  produced it, the next integrand varies on the scale of the next kernel),
  and r scales it relative to its default of 18: n = ceil((8 + 3 W/sig)
  r / 18). All work storage remains the fixed-size stack arrays used before.
* A trailing `method` argument on gsbound(), gsbound1(), probrej() and
  gsdensity() (registered in init.c), passed by the R wrappers from
  gsQuadratureMethod(), which reads options(gsDesign.quadrature = "jt"
  (default) or "gl"). Every computation built on these routines follows the
  option; normalGrid() always returns the Jennison & Turnbull grid.
* Tests (tests/testthat/test-numint-gl.R): exact multivariate normal
  references at 2e-9, agreement with the default grid within its accuracy,
  self-consistency under r = 18 vs 60 for 20 analyses and closely spaced
  analyses, infinite bounds, empty continuation regions, a near-duplicate
  analysis (information 1, 1 + eps, 3) reproducing the two-analysis design,
  and the density integrating to the continuation probability.
* Documentation: a "Numerical integration" section in ?gsDesign; NEWS.

Accuracy against exact probabilities is 1e-12 to 1e-14 (default grid: about
1e-7) with 17 to 80 nodes per analysis instead of 213, and designs that the
default grid cannot derive at r = 18 (for example gsDesign(k = 50)) are
derived normally. C-level timings are 4 to 9 times faster than 3.11.0.

